# Vectis Plugin - Crux Cross-Platform Development

## Overview

This plugin generates cross-platform applications using the Crux framework (Rust), SwiftUI iOS shells, and Kotlin/Jetpack Compose Android shells. Each shell writer reads `tokens.yaml` and `assets.yaml` directly and emits shell-local theme + asset code under its own tree. Requirements come from Specify artifacts (specs + design.md); this plugin handles code generation, testing, and review.

**Purpose**: Generate, test, and review Crux applications from structured Specify artifacts.

## Plugin Architecture

```
/plugins/vectis/
├── .cursor-plugin/
│   └── plugin.json          # Plugin manifest
├── rules/
│   └── vectis.mdc           # This file
└── skills/
    ├── core-writer/          # Specify artifacts → Rust Crux shared crate
    ├── core-reviewer/        # AI-powered code review for Crux core
    ├── test-writer/          # Spec-traced test suites for Crux core
    ├── ios-writer/           # Crux core → SwiftUI iOS shell
    ├── ios-reviewer/         # AI-powered code review for iOS shell
    ├── android-writer/       # Crux core → Kotlin/Jetpack Compose Android shell
    ├── android-reviewer/     # AI-powered code review for Android shell
    ├── image-layout-inferer/ # Reconstruct layout.yaml from screenshot images
    └── template-updater/     # Fix CLI templates + pins when upstream bumps break a fresh scaffold
```

Each skill has its own SKILL.md with full details. The Specify artifact format (specs + design.md) is described in [specify.md](../../references/specify.md).

## Common Patterns

### New Crux Application

A Crux app is defined as a single feature with platforms declared in the proposal. A feature like `todo-app` with platforms `core, ios, android` produces one spec covering all behavioral requirements (core logic plus platform-specific sections), one design document, and one task list. The build phase invokes the appropriate skills in order: core-writer, then shell writers (ios-writer, android-writer).

### Feature Update

Updated feature specs and design generate targeted changes across all affected platforms: modified Event variants and `update()` logic in the core, updated screen views in iOS and Android, and regenerated tokens if the design system changed. All changes flow from a single feature spec.

### Adding a Platform

When a new platform is added, the feature spec gains a new platform section (e.g., `## Android Shell Requirements`), the design gains a platform details section, and the build phase invokes the new platform's writer skill. Existing specs and code are unaffected.

### Design System Update

Token changes in `tokens.yaml` are picked up by each shell writer, which emits shell-local theme code under `iOS/<App>/Theme/` (iOS) and `Android/.../ui/theme/` (Android). Token changes can be part of a feature change or a standalone feature.

## Troubleshooting

### Generated Core Incomplete

- Are specs complete with requirements and scenarios?
- Does design.md include the full Crux type system (Model, Event, ViewModel, Effect)?
- Are capabilities explicitly listed in the design?

### iOS Shell Build Fails

- Does the core exist with `shared/src/app.rs`?
- Run `specify vectis verify --dir $PROJECT_DIR` -- this checks version consistency, cargo-swift compatibility, and the build pipeline end-to-end.
- If verify passes but the iOS app panics with "UniFFI contract version mismatch", the installed cargo-swift version is incompatible with the pinned UniFFI contract. Run `specify vectis update-versions --dry-run` to see expected tool versions and reinstall a compatible cargo-swift release with `cargo install cargo-swift@{version}`.

### Android Shell Build Fails

- Does the core exist with `shared/src/app.rs`?
- Was `make build` run to generate Kotlin types and UniFFI bindings?
- Is `org.gradle.java.home` set to Java 21 in `gradle.properties`?
- Does the `Application` class set `System.setProperty("uniffi.component.shared.libraryOverride", "shared")` before any UniFFI class is loaded?
- Are Rust Android targets installed? (`rustup target list --installed | grep android`)

### Tests Don't Match Expected Behavior

- Are spec scenarios accurate?
- Were requirements changed after generation?

